paper/main.bbl:38:  Kim, Zhao, and Sch{\"u}tze]{modarressi2025nolima}
paper/main.bbl:48:\newblock Large language models can be easily distracted by irrelevant context.
paper/OUTLINE.md:21:- End with four plain questions: context amount, target position, filler quality, and
paper/OUTLINE.md:22:  the paired keyword-pruning mitigation.
paper/OUTLINE.md:29:- Use `liu2023lost` for position effects and `levy2024same` for input length.
paper/OUTLINE.md:30:- Use `shi2023distracted` for irrelevant context.
paper/OUTLINE.md:31:- Use `hsieh2024ruler` and `modarressi2025nolima` for hard retrieval evaluation.
paper/OUTLINE.md:40:- Define target clause, distractor clause, literal and paraphrased questions, and filler types.
paper/OUTLINE.md:41:- Generate a scenario family independently of target position, then move only the target
paper/OUTLINE.md:42:  clause across start, middle, and end. This makes the position result paired.
paper/OUTLINE.md:43:- Balance relevant and irrelevant filler within every tier used for a quality comparison.
paper/OUTLINE.md:44:- Place distractors independently of target position.
paper/OUTLINE.md:49:- Define exact-answer scoring, distractor capture, invalid output, and exact-output compliance.
paper/OUTLINE.md:50:- Report per-cell n and Wilson intervals for every accuracy estimate.
paper/OUTLINE.md:54:- Quality: compare matched cases at the same amount, position, and question wording.
paper/OUTLINE.md:55:- Mitigation: compute the pruning gain only over scenarios having both full and pruned runs.
paper/OUTLINE.md:61:- Use no more tables than the evidence needs. Prefer one central paired-position table,
paper/OUTLINE.md:62:  one amount/quality table with explicit n, and one mitigation/baseline table.
